2026-06-30 15:50:43,699 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-91c40905-a121-40cc-abbf-6bf13790603a', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n24224_Ecl01_Anton_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_BSA_hetero_1.raw\n24224_Ecl01_Anton_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_BSA_hetero_2.raw\n24224_Ecl_DDA_IT500_TRAP_50cm_100ng_65min_BSA_oNBA_Methoxy_2.raw\n24224_Ecl_DDA_IT500_TRAP_50cm_100ng_65min_BSA_oNBA_Methoxy_3.raw\n24224_Ecl_DDA_IT500_TRAP_50cm_100ng_65min_BSA_oNBA_methoxy_1.raw\n24224_Ecl_DDA_IT500_TRAP_50cm_100ng_95min_BSA_oNBA_1.raw\n24224_Ecl_DDA_IT500_TRAP_50cm_100ng_95min_BSA_oNBA_2.raw\n24224_Ecl_DDA_IT500_TRAP_50cm_100ng_95min_BSA_oNBA_3.raw\n25001_Ecl01_Adam_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_BSA_Hetero_3.raw\n25001_Ecl01_Adam_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_BSA_Homo.raw\n25001_Ecl01_Adam_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_BSA_Homo_2.raw\n25001_Ecl01_Adam_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_BSA_Homo_3.raw\n25001_Ecl01_Adam_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_SurAOmpX_Hetero_paper.raw\n25001_Ecl01_Adam_BS3_XL_TRAP_50cm_65min_SurAOmpX_Homo_paper.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nDesign and characterisation of photoactivatable and lysine reactive o-nitrobenzyl alcohol-based crosslinkers†\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPhotoreactive groups are invaluable tools in structural proteomics, offering reagent-free activation and temporal control of protein labelling. However, traditional UV-activatable functional groups often produce unstable intermediates and diverse products, making these chemistries difficult to deploy at scale. In this study, we performed a systematic analysis of ortho-nitrobenzyl alcohol (oNBA) reactivity for integration into novel reagents for chemical crosslinking-mass spectrometry. oNBA photochemistry represents a promising alternative to traditional photoactivatable crosslinkers due to its unique specificity towards lysine residues. Here, we synthesised two molecules containing oNBA functional groups with different substituents and assessed their labelling efficiency against a model protein. To ensure high labelling yields while maintaining a short irradiation time, we constructed a high power 365 nm irradiation device which improves the efficiency of oNBA photolysis. Our studies identified an amide-substituted probe that labels proteins with high efficiency. We next incorporated this optimised oNBA moiety into a homo-bifunctional crosslinker and a hetero-bifunctional crosslinker in combination with an NHS ester, which both resulted in high yields of crosslinked products. Our findings highlight that optimised oNBA-based reactive groups are viable UV-activated warheads that can deliver high labelling yields and efficient protein crosslinking, unlocking a wealth of potential structural proteomics applications.\nNew protein crosslinking reagents are needed to enable novel crosslinking-mass spectrometry based studies. Here, we characterise photoreactive o-nitrobenzyl alcohols for both protein covalent labelling and crosslinking-mass spectrometry applications.\n\n[METHODS]\nExperimental\nOrganic synthesis\nAll reactions were conducted under an atmosphere of nitrogen and protected from light using aluminium foil. All solvents were purchased from Fisher Scientific at HPLC grade and reagents were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich, Fluorochem, Thermo Fisher and Merck Millipore. Analytical thin layer chromatography (TLC) was conducted using Merck 0.25 mm silica gel pre-coated aluminium plates (Merck silica 2880 gel, 60 F254) and visualised using UV-light. Flash column chromatography was carried out with silica gel 60 (35–75 μm particles). Solvents were removed using a Büchi rotary evaporator and a Vacuubrand PC2001 Vario Diaphragm pump. 1H and 13C NMR spectra were acquired on a Bruker AV-NEO NMR spectrometer at 500 MHz for 1H or 101 MHz for 13C. Chemical shifts are expressed as parts per million using solvent as an internal standard (DMSO-d6 2.50 ppm in 1H and 39.52 ppm in 13C spectra) and coupling constants are expressed in Hz. The following abbreviations are used: s for singlet, d for doublet, t for triplet, q for quartet, p for pentet, m for multiplet and br for broad. NMR spectra can be found in the ESI.†\nSynthesis of 4-(hydroxymethyl)-3-nitro-N-2-propyn-1-ylbenzamide (1a)\n4-(Hydroxymethyl)-3-nitrobenzoic acid (100 mg, 0.507 mmol), 1-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-3-ethylcarbodiimide hydrochloride (150 mg, 0.783 mmol) and hydroxybenzotriazole (135 mg, 1 mmol) were dissolved in CH2Cl2 (20 mL). The reaction was stirred at room temperature for 30 min before propargylamine (220 μL, 3.4 mmol) and N,N-diisopropylethylamine (1 mL, 5.74 mmol) were added. The reaction was left stirring overnight at room temperature. The solvent was then removed in vacuo to yield a brown solid. The crude product was purified by silica gel chromatography (1\u2009:\u20091 ethyl acetate\u2009:\u2009hexane) and collected fractions were concentrated to produce a pale yellow solid (77 mg, 0.329 mmol, 66%, Rf: 0.25 (1\u2009:\u20091 ethyl acetate\u2009:\u2009hexane)).\n 1H NMR (500 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm 3.17 (t, J = 2.5 Hz, 1H, H–C15), 4.09 (dd, J = 2.5, 5.5 Hz, 2H, H–C7), 4.88 (d, J = 5.5 Hz, 2H, H–C13), 5.67 (t, J = 5.5 Hz, 1H, OH), 7.95 (d, J = 8.1 Hz, 1H, H–C4), 8.23 (dd, J = 1.8, 8.1 Hz, 1H, H–C3), 8.53 (d, J = 1.8 Hz, 1H, H–C1), 9.27 (t, J = 5.4 Hz, 1H, NH). 13C NMR (101 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm: 28.7 (C13), 59.9 (C7), 73.2 (C14/15), 80.9 (C14/15), 123.2 (CAr), 128.6 (CAr), 132.2 (CAr),133.2 (CAr), 141.7 (CAr), 146.6 (CAr), 163.8 (C10).\nSynthesis of (2-nitro-5-(prop-2-yn-1-yloxy)phenyl)methanol (1b)\n5-hydroxy-2-nitrobenzyl alcohol (169 mg, 0.998 mmol) and anhydrous potassium carbonate (2.76 mg, 0.019 mmol) were added to 1.5 mL of anhydrous DMF and the resulting mixture was stirred for 2 hours at 60 °C. Then, propargyl bromide (0.15 mL, 2 mmol) was slowly added into the reaction and the orange mixture was stirred for 24 hours at room temperature. After the reaction was complete the solvent was removed under reduced pressure and the residue was resuspended in ethyl acetate (10 mL). The suspension was then washed once with water (10 mL) and three times with brine (10 mL). After this, the organic layer was dried over MgSO4 and filtered. The solvent was then removed under reduced pressure and the residue purified via flash column chromatography on silica gel (1\u2009:\u20091 ethyl acetate\u2009:\u2009hexane). The sample was concentrated to dryness to deliver a yellow solid (123 mg, 0.594 mmol, 59%, Rf: 0.34 (1\u2009:\u20091 ethyl acetate\u2009:\u2009hexane)).\n 1H NMR (500 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm: 3.67 (t, J = 2.4 Hz, 1H, H–C12), 4.85 (d, J = 5.5 Hz, 2H, H–C7), 4.97 (d, J = 2.4, 2H, H–C10), 5.59 (t, J = 5.5 Hz, 1H, H–C8), 7.09 (dd, J = 2.9, 9.1 Hz, 1H, H–C5), 7.41 (d, J = 2.9 Hz, 1H, H–C3), 8.15 (d, J = 9.1 Hz, 1H, H–C6). 13C NMR (101 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm: 56.14 (C7), 60.1(C10), 78.4 (C12), 79.1 (C11), 113.1 (CAr), 113.8 (CAr), 127.4 (CAr), 139.9 (CAr), 142.2 (CAr), 161.5 (CAr).\nSynthesis of 2,5-dioxo-1-pyrrolidinyl-4-(hydroxymethyl)-3-nitrobenzoate (2)\n4-(Hydroxymethyl)-3-nitrobenzoic acid (100 mg, 0.507 mmol), N-hydroxysuccinimide (120 mg, 1.043 mmol) and 1-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-3-ethylcarbodiimide hydrochloride (125 mg, 0.805 mmol) were added to a round bottom flask. DMF (5 mL) was added, and the resulting orange solution was stirred overnight at room temperature. The reaction mixture, deionised water (20 mL) and ethyl acetate (20 mL) were added to a separating funnel and the phases were separated. The aqueous layer was removed, and the organic layer was washed three times with water (20 mL) and then dried over MgSO4, filtered and concentrated to yield a yellow solid. The crude product was purified by silica gel chromatography (2\u2009:\u20091 ethyl acetate\u2009:\u2009hexane) to yield the title compound as a white-yellow solid (80 mg, 0.272 mmol, 53%, Rf: 0.28 (2\u2009:\u20091 ethyl acetate\u2009:\u2009hexane)).\n 1H NMR (500 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm: 2.91 (s, 4H, H–C15H–C16), 4.96 (d, J = 5.5 Hz, 2H, H–C7), 5.84 (t, J = 5.5 Hz, 1H, H–C8), 8.15 (d, J = 8.2 Hz, 1H, H–C3), 8.46 (dd, J = 1.8, 8.2 Hz, 1H, H–C4), 8.63 (d, J = 1.8 Hz, 1H, H–C6). 13C NMR (101 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm: 25.6 (C15C16), 60.0 (C7), 123.8 (CAr), 125.9 (CAr), 129.7 (CAr), 134.5 (CAr), 146.2 (CAr), 146.9 (CAr), 160.4 (C10), 170.1 (C14C17).\nSynthesis of N,N′-1,2-ethanediylbis(4-hydroxymethyl-3-nitrobenzamide) (3)\n4-(Hydroxymethyl)-3-nitrobenzoic acid (290 mg, 1.47 mmol), 1-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-3-ethylcarbodiimide hydrochloride (325 mg, 1.69 mmol), and N-hydroxybenzotriazole (230 mg, 1.69 mmol) were added to a flask and flushed with N2. The mixture was then dissolved in acetonitrile (20 mL) and left to stir for 30 min at room temperature. Ethylenediamine was then added (270 μL, 0.43 mmol) as well as DIPEA (300 μL, 1.69 mmol) and the reaction was left to stir overnight at room temperature. Once the reaction was complete, the mixture was gravity filtered and the solution was dried in vacuo. The crude product was dissolved in 10 mL of a 1\u2009:\u20091 mixture of acetonitrile and water and purified using preparative HPLC on an Agilent 1260 infinity system equipped with UV detector and fraction collector on a Kinetex EVO 5 μm C18 100 A 21.2 × 250 mm reverse phase column. The solution (4.5 mL) was injected, and a 25 min gradient of 20–50% acetonitrile in water with 0.1% formic acid was performed at 15 mL min−1. The fractions containing the product were combined and freeze dried to afford the title compound as a white solid (10 mg, 24 μmol, 6%).\n 1H NMR (500 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm 3.43–3.56 (m, 4H, H–C13), 4.87 (d, J = 5.5 Hz, 4H, H–C7), 5.65 (t, J = 5.5 Hz, 2H, H–C8), 7.93 (d, J = 8.1 Hz, 2H, H–C3), 8.21 (dd, J = 1.8, 8.1 Hz, 2H, H–C4), 8.51 (d, J = 1.8 Hz, 2H, H–C6), 8.93 (s, 2H, 2× NH). 13C NMR (101 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ/ppm: 60.4 (C7), 123.6 (CAr), 128.9 (CAr), 132.6 (CAr), 134.5 (CAr), 141.8 (CAr), 147.0 (CAr), 164.8 (C10).\nSurA expression and purification\nThis protocol was adapted from previous work. A pET28b plasmid containing the mature SurA sequence (without the signal peptide sequence) preceded by an N-terminal 6× His-tag and a TEV-cleavage site was transformed into BL21(DE3) cells (Stratagene). Cells were grown in LB medium supplemented with 30 μg per mL kanamycin at 37 °C with shaking (200 rpm) until an OD600 of ∼0.6 was reached. The temperature was subsequently lowered to 20 °C, and expression induced with 0.4 mM IPTG. After ∼18 h, cells were harvested by centrifugation (4000 × g, 15 min), resuspended in 25 mM Tris–HCl, pH 7.2, 150 mM NaCl, 20 mM imidazole, containing EDTA-free protease inhibitor tablets (Roche), and lysed using a cell disrupter (Constant Cell Disruption Systems). The cell debris was removed by centrifugation (20 min, 4 °C, 39\u2009000 × g), and the lysate was applied to a 5 mL HisTrap column (Cytiva). The column was washed with 25 mM Tris–HCl, pH 7.2, 150 mM NaCl and 20 mM imidazole, and SurA was eluted with 25 mM Tris–HCl, 150 mM NaCl, 500 mM imidazole, pH 7.2. The eluate was dialysed against 25 mM Tris–HCl, 150 mM NaCl, pH 8.0 overnight, and the following day TEV protease (ca. 0.5 mg) and 0.1% (v/v) β-mercaptoethanol were added. The cleavage reaction was left to proceed overnight at 4 °C on a tube \n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nEscherichia coli; Bos taurus (bovine)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
